(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-quiz"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which laboratory abnormalities are common in erysipelas, and what do they contribute?","choices":["Leukocytosis with raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein — common but not diagnostic","Thrombocytopenia with mild hyponatraemia and transaminitis — and diagnostic in almost every case","Eosinophilia with a raised immunoglobulin E — diagnostic when combined with the rash","Entirely normal inflammatory markers — which is what confirms the diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis remains clinical in a classic presentation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"How is recurrent folliculitis in a Staphylococcus aureus carrier treated?","choices":["Topical clindamycin applied twice daily to the affected follicles for a month","Mupirocin ointment applied in the nasal vestibule twice daily for five days","Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole taken twice daily for a ten-day course","Chlorhexidine washes to the affected areas daily for a two-week period"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carriage of the organism in the nose sustains recurrence. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What are the mandatory safety requirements for oral isotretinoin?","choices":["Weekly complete blood counts throughout the course, with pregnancy testing left to clinical judgement","Baseline electrocardiogram and monthly potassium levels, with contraception advised but not required","Negative pregnancy tests before, monthly during and five weeks after treatment, with iPledge enrolment","Negative pregnancy tests before treatment only, with liver function checked at the end of the course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Only one month is dispensed at a time and blood donation is prohibited. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How is chronic paronychia treated?","choices":["Topical mupirocin applied to the nail fold three times daily until the swelling settles","Keep the hands dry and use a broad-spectrum topical antifungal, with oral fluconazole in severe cases","Warm soaks three times daily with incision and drainage if purulent material has collected","Oral cephalexin for ten days with removal of the nail plate if there is no early response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treat the underlying inflammation and infection together. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which statement about preventing hot tub folliculitis is correct?","choices":["Raising the water temperature above forty degrees kills the organism responsible","Wearing a rash guard in the water prevents the organism reaching the follicles","Showering after contact does not prevent it; continuous filtration and disinfectant monitoring do","Showering immediately after contact reliably prevents it if done within thirty minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, along with frequent changing of the water. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which acne lesions are classed as non-inflammatory?","choices":["Open comedones (blackheads) and closed comedones (whiteheads)","Papules and pustules arising on a background of facial erythema","Nodules and cysts that heal with atrophic or hypertrophic scars","Telangiectasias and persistent flushing of the central cheeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Papules, pustules and nodules make up the inflammatory group. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"When should cultures be obtained in impetigo?","choices":["In every case at first presentation, since staphylococcal and streptococcal disease are treated differently from one another","Only when the lesions have failed to respond after a full two-week course of topical mupirocin","Only in children under three years of age, since they are the group at highest risk of complications","When the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, or when post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a health-care worker or teacher is the example given of high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How does the sex distribution of acne differ between adolescence and adulthood?","choices":["Adolescent acne is more common and severe in females; post-adolescent acne is more common in men","Adolescent acne affects both sexes equally; post-adolescent acne is far more common in men","Adolescent acne is more common in males; post-adolescent acne affects both sexes about equally","Adolescent acne is more common and severe in males; post-adolescent acne is more common in women"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Post-adolescent acne is defined here as acne over 25 years of age. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which shaving advice is given for pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["Shave daily against the grain with a multi-blade razor to cut hairs below the surface","Shave weekly with an electric razor set to the closest possible cutting length","Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical antibiotic","Stop shaving if possible, use clean razors, and avoid lift-and-cut razor systems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mild razor angles and single or at most double blades are preferred. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which lesion type is characteristic of hidradenitis suppurativa on examination?","choices":["The double comedone — a blackhead with two or more openings","The honey-coloured adherent crust overlying a superficial erosion","The follicular pustule pierced by a single central hair shaft","The tense clear bulla that leaves a collarette after rupture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside nodules, sinus tracts, abscesses and meshlike scarring. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What distinguishes ecthyma from ordinary impetigo?","choices":["It deepens into a dermal ulceration with a thicker grey-yellow crust, heals slowly and scars","It remains confined to the epidermis, resolves within a week and never leaves a scar behind","It produces tense bullae on intact skin that rupture to leave shallow moist erosions","It spreads rapidly along superficial lymphatics with a sharply demarcated raised edge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, usually on the lower extremities, with regional lymphadenopathy common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is described as the drug of choice for impetigo in children?","choices":["Dicloxacillin","Erythromycin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What is the defining physical finding of erysipelas?","choices":["A honey-coloured crust firmly adherent over a superficial epidermal erosion","A plaque raised above the level of surrounding skin with a clear line of demarcation","An area of erythema whose borders are neither elevated nor sharply demarcated","A firm tender nodule with a single central opening and surrounding erythema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lower extremities are involved in 80% of patients, with red streaks toward lymph nodes. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What is a carbuncle?","choices":["A single furuncle larger than five millimetres across","A furuncle that has become secondarily infected with yeast","A furuncle that has failed to respond to oral antibiotics","Two or more confluent furuncles with separate heads"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — interconnecting furuncles arising in several hair follicles. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the pharmacologic treatment of erysipelas?","choices":["Mupirocin ointment, with oral dicloxacillin if lesions are widespread","Doxycycline, with benzoyl peroxide added to reduce bacterial resistance","Penicillin V, with clindamycin if the patient is penicillin allergic","Cephalexin, with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole if purulence is present"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prompt treatment matters because progression can be rapid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which complication may follow impetigo, and can antibiotics prevent it?","choices":["Toxic shock syndrome, which topical mupirocin has been shown to prevent","Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, which antibiotics do not prevent","Acute rheumatic fever, which prompt antibiotic treatment reliably prevents","Necrotizing fasciitis, which early oral antibiotics prevent in most cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the immune activation usually precedes antibiotic treatment. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which four factors underlie acne vulgaris?","choices":["Androgen deficiency, keratin thinning, fungal overgrowth and vascular ectasia","Follicular hyperkeratinisation, increased sebum, Cutibacterium acnes and inflammation","Follicular atrophy, reduced sebum, Staphylococcus aureus and lymphatic obstruction","Apocrine duct occlusion, friction, secondary infection and follicular scarring"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The temporal sequence between the four is not fully understood. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why will antibiotics not work on the devitalised area in a cellulitic limb?","choices":["The organism in devitalised tissue is always methicillin resistant","Devitalised tissue destroys antibiotic molecules on contact","Oral antibiotics never reach the subcutaneous tissue at all","It is not perfused, so the drug never reaches the site"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed or blanched tissue needs debridement. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for erysipelas?","choices":["Puberty, androgen excess, a first-degree family history, lithium and systemic corticosteroids","Impaired lymphatic drainage, immunocompromise, tinea pedis, obesity, trauma and pre-existing impetigo","Repeated hand immersion in water, occupational chemical contact, diabetes and nail biting","Hot weather, excessive perspiration, apocrine duct obstruction, obesity and cigarette smoking"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mastectomy is the example given of impaired lymphatic drainage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["Mastectomy, tinea pedis, obesity and any pre-existing superficial skin infection","Manicure, hangnail, ingrown nail and the habit of biting or picking at the nails","Trauma, burns, surgery, immunosuppression, renal failure, alcoholism, dental infection and injection drug use","Obesity, poor hygiene, occlusive clothing, hot humid weather and nasal staphylococcal carriage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it occurs more often in males than females. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"What is the expected course of cellulitis on antibiotic therapy?","choices":["It may look worse on day one, fever usually resolves within twenty-four hours, inflammation over one to two weeks","It improves visibly within hours, fever resolves within six hours, and erythema clears within three days","It worsens for the first week before any improvement, with fever expected to persist throughout","It resolves without any change in appearance, since the erythema is not caused by the infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fever beyond forty-eight hours prompts a change guided by culture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which patients are given as examples of high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus in the impetigo section?","choices":["A health-care worker or a teacher","A child under three or an infant","A swimmer or a laundry worker","A bartender or a professional cook"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Culture is obtained in those patients rather than in every case. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"By what mechanisms do oral contraceptive pills improve acne?","choices":["Estrogen suppresses Cutibacterium acnes directly and reduces follicular hyperkeratinisation","Estrogen decreases sebum, suppresses ovarian androgens and raises sex hormone binding globulin","Estrogen increases sebum, stimulates ovarian androgens and lowers sex hormone binding globulin","Progestin blocks the androgen receptor within the sebaceous gland and reduces keratinisation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Raising that globulin lowers circulating free testosterone. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for a carbuncle?","choices":["Incision and drainage, with endocarditis prophylaxis for at-risk patients","Warm compresses alone, since most carbuncles drain without intervention","Oral antibiotics alone, since drainage risks seeding the bloodstream","Wide surgical excision of the affected area under general anaesthesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside oral antibiotics chosen for the likely organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the primary portal of entry for impetigo?","choices":["Minor superficial breaks in the skin such as cuts and bug bites","An occluded apocrine duct that ruptures into the surrounding dermis","A hair follicle already inflamed by shaving or occlusive clothing","Traumatic inoculation of bacteria deep into the dermal tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. No predisposing lesion is identified in most patients. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is specifically suggested for paronychia after nail biting?","choices":["Doxycycline, because of exposure to oral flora","Ciprofloxacin, because of exposure to oral flora","Penicillin V, because of exposure to oral flora","Clindamycin, because of exposure to oral flora"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin-clavulanate and cephalexin are the other named options. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"How is acute paronychia treated?","choices":["Oral antibiotics alone in all cases, since incision of the nail fold risks permanent deformity","Warm soaks for twenty minutes three times daily in mild cases; incision and drainage in severe cases","A broad-spectrum topical antifungal in all cases, with oral fluconazole if the disease is severe","Immediate nail plate removal in all cases, since the nail harbours the responsible organism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with oral antibiotics and cultures to rule out resistant organisms where appropriate. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"How is a cutaneous abscess treated?","choices":["Oral antibiotics alone in every case, since incision risks spreading the infection into the blood","Warm compresses alone in every case, since abscesses reliably resolve without any intervention","Wide surgical excision of the affected area, since recurrence is otherwise close to universal","Surgical incision and drainage if it does not drain spontaneously; warm soaks and antibiotics if it does"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum cover considers methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, then narrows to culture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which organism most commonly causes bacterial folliculitis?","choices":["Corynebacterium minutissimum, with Candida albicans occasionally present","Cutibacterium acnes, with Staphylococcus epidermidis occasionally involved","Staphylococcus aureus, with Pseudomonas aeruginosa occasionally responsible","Streptococcus pyogenes, with Staphylococcus aureus occasionally responsible"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Folliculitis can also be fungal, viral, physical or chemical in origin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which organism causes erythrasma and where does it live?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa, entering the follicle from inadequately chlorinated water","Staphylococcus aureus, releasing epidermolytic toxins that split the epidermis","Corynebacterium minutissimum, invading the upper third of the stratum corneum","Candida albicans, colonising the space beneath a separated proximal nail fold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heat and humidity are the favourable conditions; diabetics are high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 80"}]};
